{
  "id": "Compound::DB00741 - Disease::DOID:8893",
  "verbalization": "Hydrocortisone is implicated in the pathogenesis of Psoriasis through a shared mechanism involving Betamethasone, a corticosteroid that acts as a therapeutic agent for the condition. The evidence suggests that Hydrocortisone causes a range of adverse effects, including Adrenocortical Insufficiency Acute, Proximal Myopathy, and Pseudotumor, all of which are mediated by Betamethasone. Betamethasone, in turn, is known to treat Psoriasis, indicating a potential causal link between the corticosteroid and the disease. The lowest common ancestor of these relationships is the 17Alpha-Hydroxy Steroid, highlighting a common biological pathway. The integration of these findings suggests that Hydrocortisone may exert a pathogenic role in Psoriasis through the activation of a corticosteroid signaling cascade, potentially leading to immune dysregulation and skin inflammation. The evidence supports a mechanistic connection, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
